  "meta": {
    "title": "Rising-edge detector (Moore)",
    "blurb": "The same pulse as the Mealy detector, from a machine whose outputs live on its states. It needs a third state, “edge”, that exists only to say 1 — and, as always with Moore, an extra output for the start state.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "0110",
        "out": "00100"
      },
      {
        "w": "1010",
        "out": "01010"
      },
      {
        "w": "0111001",
        "out": "00100001"
      },
      {
        "w": "1111",
        "out": "01000"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "out": "0",
        "label": "nothing read"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "hardware",
        "edge-detector",
        "moore-vs-mealy",
        "sequential-circuit"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "A Moore machine's output is a function of its state alone, so \"the input just went from 0 to 1\" has to be a *place* the machine can be. That is the **edge** state: entered on a 1 from **low**, left on the very next input.\n\nThe output string is one symbol longer than the input, because the start state speaks before anything is read: **0110** gives **00100**. Drop that first symbol and it is exactly the Mealy machine's output. In hardware terms the Moore output is glitch-free and registered, which is why designers accept the extra state."
    }
  }
}
